You are assisting with business process modelling. A first check could not decide whether the conditions in the list below hold for the business process described below. They are conditions of the rule [GDPR-Art13-1]. Look again.

INSTRUCTIONS
- The legal passages below are all the rules found for this process. Use them only to understand what a condition means (for example a definition of a word). Whether a condition holds must still be shown by the process description.
- For each condition, choose its status:
  - "confirmed": the description states that it holds, in the same or different words.
  - "contradicted": the description states something that makes it false.
  - "not_stated": the description does not say whether it holds.
- Do not guess facts the description does not state. If in doubt, use "not_stated".
- quote: for "confirmed" or "contradicted", copy the words of the process description that show it, exactly and word for word. For "not_stated", use null. Quotes are checked automatically; a quote that does not appear in the description counts as "not_stated".
- Give one result for every condition, with its id, and no other results.
- Answer with one JSON object that follows the JSON schema below. Output only the JSON, with no other text.

JSON SCHEMA
{
  "$defs": {
    "ItemCheck": {
      "properties": {
        "id": {
          "description": "The id of the item, as given in the list",
          "title": "Id",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "status": {
          "description": "confirmed, contradicted or not_stated, judged from the description only",
          "enum": [
            "confirmed",
            "not_stated",
            "contradicted"
          ],
          "title": "Status",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "quote": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Exact words copied from the process description, or null for not_stated",
          "title": "Quote"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "id",
        "status",
        "quote"
      ],
      "title": "ItemCheck",
      "type": "object"
    }
  },
  "properties": {
    "results": {
      "description": "One result for every item of the list",
      "items": {
        "$ref": "#/$defs/ItemCheck"
      },
      "title": "Results",
      "type": "array"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "results"
  ],
  "title": "CheckOutputV2",
  "type": "object"
}

PROCESS DESCRIPTION
This process begins when a potential customer inquires about a product or service.
Sales staff or customer support collects relevant information and addresses any concerns or questions.
If the customer is interested, they are guided through selecting the appropriate product or service.
Next, the sales representative provides a quote, and after approval from the customer, the process moves to order placement.
The order is then recorded in the system, and the customer receives confirmation of their order.
The process ends when the order is successfully placed and confirmed.

LEGAL PASSAGES
[ECD-Art11-2] e-Commerce Directive (2000/31/EC) - Article 11: Placing of the order
2. Member States shall ensure that, except when otherwise agreed by parties who are not consumers, the service provider makes available to the recipient of the service appropriate, effective and accessible technical means allowing him to identify and correct input errors, prior to the placing of the order.

[ECD-Art11-1] e-Commerce Directive (2000/31/EC) - Article 11: Placing of the order
1. Member States shall ensure, except when otherwise agreed by parties who are not consumers, that in cases where the recipient of the service places his order through technological means, the following principles apply:
- the service provider has to acknowledge the receipt of the recipient's order without undue delay and by electronic means,
- the order and the acknowledgement of receipt are deemed to be received when the parties to whom they are addressed are able to access them.

[ECD-Art10-1] e-Commerce Directive (2000/31/EC) - Article 10: Information to be provided
1. In addition to other information requirements established by Community law, Member States shall ensure, except when otherwise agreed by parties who are not consumers, that at least the following information is given by the service provider clearly, comprehensibly and unambiguously and prior to the order being placed by the recipient of the service:
(a) the different technical steps to follow to conclude the contract;
(b) whether or not the concluded contract will be filed by the service provider and whether it will be accessible;
(c) the technical means for identifying and correcting input errors prior to the placing of the order;
(d) the languages offered for the conclusion of the contract.

[CRD-Art8-3] Consumer Rights Directive (2011/83/EU) - Article 8: Formal requirements for distance contracts
3. Trading websites shall indicate clearly and legibly at the latest at the beginning of the ordering process whether any delivery restrictions apply and which means of payment are accepted.

[GDPR-Art13-1] General Data Protection Regulation (2016/679) - Article 13: Information to be provided where personal data are collected from the data subject
1. Where personal data relating to a data subject are collected from the data subject, the controller shall, at the time when personal data are obtained, provide the data subject with all of the following information:
(a) the identity and the contact details of the controller and, where applicable, of the controller's representative;
(b) the contact details of the data protection officer, where applicable;
(c) the purposes of the processing for which the personal data are intended as well as the legal basis for the processing;
(d) where the processing is based on point (f) of Article 6(1), the legitimate interests pursued by the controller or by a third party;
(e) the recipients or categories of recipients of the personal data, if any;
(f) where applicable, the fact that the controller intends to transfer personal data to a third country or international organisation and the existence or absence of an adequacy decision by the Commission, or in the case of transfers referred to in Article 46 or 47, or the second subparagraph of Article 49(1), reference to the appropriate or suitable safeguards and the means by which to obtain a copy of them or where they have been made available.

CONDITIONS
C1: personal data relating to a data subject are collected from the data subject

